  <div class="section-label">Community Overview</div>
  <h2>West Haven's Role in Northern Davis County Growth</h2>

  <p>West Haven is an incorporated city in northern Davis County, approximately 15–20 miles north of Salt Lake City and west of Ogden. The city has grown rapidly over the past two decades, transforming from a rural farming community to a suburban hub with growing commercial and residential development.</p>

  <p>West Haven's strategic location — between the Ogden Valley (east), the Great Salt Lake (west), and the Inland Port corridor (south) — positions it as a critical location for regional growth and commerce. The City Council's economic development efforts are focused on capturing sales tax revenue, attracting commercial activity, and coordinating infrastructure with regional projects like SR-177.</p>

  <p>SR-177 Phase 3 (Weber County extension) will pass through or adjacent to West Haven's southern boundary (around 4000 South), affecting properties in the 5100 West corridor and shaping the city's long-term development patterns.</p>

  <div class="section-label">Economic Development Strategy</div>
  <h2>West Haven's Growth Plan & Regional Context</h2>

  <p>West Haven's City Council has prioritized economic development and infrastructure improvements to support regional growth. Key strategic initiatives include:</p>

  <ul style="margin: 12px 0 16px 24px; line-height: 1.8;">
    <li><strong>Sales Tax Revenue Capture</strong> — West Haven aims to capture sales tax from regional shopping and commerce. The city has identified "sales leakage" (revenue flowing to neighboring cities) and is working to retain growth within city limits.{% cite "whc-rob-sant-economic" %}</li>
    <li><strong>Local Road Infrastructure Improvements</strong> — The city is investing in roadway widening and capacity improvements on key corridors like 5100 West to support traffic flow and commercial access.{% cite "whc-res-16-5100w-bid" %}</li>
    <li><strong>Parks and Recreation Master Planning</strong> — West Haven has contracted professional services to develop a master park plan, leveraging trail connectivity from SR-177 and regional recreation opportunities.{% cite "whc-res-09-park-plan" %}</li>
    <li><strong>Coordination with Regional Projects</strong> — The city is coordinating SR-177 planning, the West Davis Corridor extension, and Inland Port development to maximize economic benefit.{% cite "whc-warnke-minutes" %}</li>
  </ul>

  <p><strong>Why this matters for property owners:</strong> West Haven's economic development focus means the city may support SR-177 approval and accelerated construction timelines if they believe the highway will stimulate regional growth and sales tax revenue. Property owners should be aware that the city council's interests may not align with property owner concerns about land loss, noise, or relocation costs.</p>

  <div class="section-label">West Haven City Council Actions (2026)</div>
  <h2>Resolutions and Governance Decisions This Year</h2>

  <p>The West Haven City Council has passed several resolutions in 2026 that reflect the city's priorities and relationship to SR-177:</p>

  <div style="margin: 24px 0; padding: 20px; background: #f0f0f0; border-left: 4px solid #b84c1e;">
    <h3 style="margin: 0 0 12px; font-size: 18px;">Resolution 09-2026: Master Park Plan Professional Services</h3>
    <p style="margin: 0 0 8px;"><strong>Date Passed:</strong> 2026</p>
    <p style="margin: 0 0 8px;"><strong>Purpose:</strong> West Haven authorized professional services to develop a comprehensive master park plan for the city.{% cite "whc-res-09-park-plan" %}</p>
    <p style="margin: 0;"><strong>Relevance to SR-177:</strong> The master park plan likely includes integration of multi-use trails from the SR-177 project (particularly the Emigrant Trail extension). Parks and trails are often used to promote regional amenities and attract commercial development. This resolution indicates West Haven is planning for infrastructure that will complement the SR-177 extension.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin: 24px 0; padding: 20px; background: #f0f0f0; border-left: 4px solid #b84c1e;">
    <h3 style="margin: 0 0 12px; font-size: 18px;">Resolution 15-2026: 3600 South Construction Authorization</h3>
    <p style="margin: 0 0 8px;"><strong>Date Passed:</strong> 2026</p>
    <p style="margin: 0 0 8px;"><strong>Purpose:</strong> West Haven authorized construction work on 3600 South, a major east-west corridor in the city.{% cite "whc-res-15-3600s" %}</p>
    <p style="margin: 0;"><strong>Relevance to SR-177:</strong> 3600 South is an important local road that may serve as a feeder route to SR-177 or support local traffic redistribution. This resolution demonstrates West Haven's commitment to local infrastructure improvements that prepare the city for regional highway connections.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin: 24px 0; padding: 20px; background: #f0f0f0; border-left: 4px solid #b84c1e;">
    <h3 style="margin: 0 0 12px; font-size: 18px;">Resolution 16-2026: 5100 West Widening Project Bid Award</h3>
    <p style="margin: 0 0 8px;"><strong>Date Passed:</strong> April 2026</p>
    <p style="margin: 0 0 8px;"><strong>Purpose:</strong> West Haven awarded a contract for the 5100 West roadway widening project, a significant local infrastructure investment.{% cite "whc-res-16-5100w-bid" %}</p>
    <p style="margin: 0;"><strong>Relevance to SR-177:</strong> This is perhaps the most direct signal of West Haven's SR-177 coordination strategy. 5100 West is the proposed SR-177 corridor alignment. By investing in 5100 West widening now, West Haven is:</p>
    <ul style="margin: 12px 0 0 24px; line-height: 1.8;">
      <li>Preparing the corridor for eventual SR-177 construction</li>
      <li>Improving local traffic flow in anticipation of highway impacts</li>
      <li>Demonstrating to UDOT that the city supports and is coordinating with the SR-177 project</li>
      <li>Potentially leveraging local investment to influence UDOT alignment and timing decisions</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="section-label">West Haven's SR-177 Position</div>
  <h2>City Council Alignment & Development Coordination</h2>

  <p><strong>Key Takeaway:</strong> West Haven City Council is actively supportive of SR-177 Phase 3 and is coordinating local infrastructure investment to align with the project.</p>

  <p>Evidence from the 2026 resolutions and council actions:</p>
  <ul style="margin: 12px 0 16px 24px; line-height: 1.8;">
    <li><strong>Proactive Infrastructure Investment</strong> — The city is investing in 5100 West, 3600 South, and parks before SR-177 is funded or construction begins. This suggests confidence in the project moving forward.</li>
    <li><strong>Economic Development Lens</strong> — The Economic Development presentation focused on sales tax capture and regional competitiveness, framing SR-177 as an opportunity for growth.</li>
    <li><strong>Public Record Statements</strong> — City officials have publicly stated that UDOT is studying an alignment along 5100 West, signaling transparency and coordination with the project.{% cite "whc-warnke-minutes" %}</li>
    <li><strong>Regional Coordination</strong> — West Haven is coordinating with regional partners on trail connectivity, parks, and infrastructure planning.</li>
  </ul>

  <p><strong>For property owners:</strong> This city council support means West Haven is unlikely to oppose SR-177 or advocate for significant alignment changes. The city may even support accelerated timelines or funding approval. Property owners in West Haven should be aware that the city council's interests (regional growth, sales tax) differ from individual property owner interests (land preservation, minimizing relocation impacts).</p>

  <div class="section-label">Property Owner Implications</div>
  <h2>What West Haven's Actions Mean for You</h2>

  <p>If you own property in or near West Haven (generally north of 4000 South, along or near 5100 West):</p>

  <ol style="margin: 12px 0 16px 24px; line-height: 2;">
    <li><strong>City's Position is Pro-Development.</strong> West Haven City Council supports SR-177 and is coordinating local improvements. The city is unlikely to advocate for property owner protections.</li>
    <li><strong>Local Infrastructure Changes Coming.</strong> Widening of 5100 West and 3600 South will happen regardless of SR-177 timing. Be prepared for construction activity and traffic patterns changes on these routes.</li>
    <li><strong>City May Support Accelerated Timelines.</strong> If UDOT offers funding options for earlier construction, West Haven may advocate for acceleration to support economic development. This could accelerate property acquisition timelines.</li>
    <li><strong>Zoning & Development Pressures.</strong> SR-177 approval may trigger zoning changes and development pressures in West Haven. Commercial and residential uses may shift in anticipation of highway access.</li>
    <li><strong>Engage City Council Early.</strong> If you have concerns about SR-177 impacts, consider attending West Haven City Council meetings to make your position known. The council has stated they are coordinating with UDOT.</li>
  </ol>

  <div class="section-label">Staying Informed</div>
  <h2>How to Track West Haven's Position & Actions</h2>

  <p><strong>City Council Meetings:</strong> West Haven City Council typically meets monthly. Agendas and meeting recordings are available on the city website (westhavencity.gov).</p>

  <p><strong>Key Officials:</strong> City council members and the economic development director are involved in SR-177 coordination. You can attend meetings to ask questions or provide public comment.</p>

  <p><strong>Resolutions & Records:</strong> All city council resolutions are public record. You can request copies from West Haven City Hall or access them through the city's website or government transparency portals.</p>

  <p><strong>Next Steps:</strong></p>
  <ul style="margin: 12px 0 16px 24px; line-height: 1.8;">
    <li>Monitor West Haven City Council meetings for updates on SR-177 coordination</li>
    <li>Review the Master Park Plan once it's complete (Resolution 09-2026) to understand planned trail connections and public facilities</li>
    <li>Watch for any city council advocacy or statements regarding SR-177 funding or timeline changes</li>
    <li>Attend public hearings when UDOT releases the Draft SES (expected Fall 2026) to see how West Haven provides input</li>
  </ul>

